WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.680
[music]

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.272
That's no symphony. 

00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:10.000
It's the sound of&nbsp;a supernova remnant called Cassiopeia&nbsp;A.&nbsp;

00:00:10.474 --> 00:00:15.885
A team of scientists and musicians has&nbsp;
created musical versions of telescope images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.028 --> 00:00:21.764
In these "sonifications", brighter objects translate&nbsp;to louder notes

00:00:21.764 --> 00:00:25.547
Objects near the top of the image&nbsp;translate to higher pitches.

00:00:25.757 --> 00:00:28.640
And different kinds&nbsp;of light are played on different instruments. 

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.000
Take this image of the center of the milky way.  

00:00:32.288 --> 00:00:35.739
We've got the violin on an optical light solo,

00:00:37.365 --> 00:00:40.360
the xylophone on an x-ray light solo,

00:00:40.811 --> 00:00:43.591
and the&nbsp;piano on an infrared light solo.

00:00:44.621 --> 00:00:49.350
All together the harmony reveals glowing clouds of gas and dust...

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.350
scattered with stars.

00:00:52.553 --> 00:00:57.271
So far scientists have cut&nbsp;tracks of some iconic telescope images.

00:00:58.421 --> 00:01:01.190
But&nbsp;each composition sounds a little different.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.439
What you're hearing now are the&nbsp;Pillars of Creation in the Eagle Nebula.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.969
Those whistles indicate x-rays from stars,

00:01:10.969 --> 00:01:14.529
and&nbsp;that low drone is one of the three pillars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.714
finger-like plumes of gas&nbsp;and dust where stars are born.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.000
These musical scores and others like them

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.458
could make astronomy more accessible for

00:01:24.458 --> 00:01:27.020
people with blindness or vision impairments,

00:01:27.478 --> 00:01:30.710
offering a new way to tour the cosmos.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.344
[music]

